<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ipatiev Monastery</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ipatiev Monastery is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, traditionally founded around 1330 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> noble Chet, ancestor of the Godunov family. In 1613 the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> was living there with his mother when envoys of the Assembly of the Land came to offer him the throne. Its library preserved the Hypatian Codex, a key chronicle of Kievan and Galician Rus'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ipatiev-monastery.jpg" alt="Ipatiev Monastery" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexxx1979 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kostroma._Ipatiev_Monastery_P7150308_2350.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The monastery is dedicated to St. Hypatios of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cankiri">Gangra</a>, and in English its name is sometimes rendered as the Hypatian Monastery. Its founder is held to have been the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> murza Chet, baptised as Zachary, whose male-line descendants included Solomonia Saburova as well as Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>. Legend says he was cured of an illness by a vision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> with St. Philip and St. Hypatius and built the monastery in gratitude. Some historians instead say that it was founded in 1275 by Yaroslavich and declined with the Kostroma Principality after his death, so that Chet may only have revived it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1435 Vasily II made peace with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-kosoy">Vasily Kosoy</a> at the monastery, then a notable centre of learning. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a> supporters of False Dmitriy II occupied it in the spring of 1609, and in September of that year the Muscovite army took it after a long siege. On 14 March 1613 the Zemsky Sobor announced that Mikhail Romanov, who was living there, would be tsar.</p><p>Most of its buildings date from the 16th and 17th centuries, among them the Trinity Cathedral with its painted interior. Konstantin Thon rebuilt the church of the Nativity of the Mother of God for Tsar Nicholas I and designed the main entrance from the riverside. Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> had Mikhail Romanov's house restored, though Konstantin Pobedonostsev questioned the authenticity of the work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The monastery was disbanded after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 and its buildings passed into the care of museums. The Soviet authorities demolished Thon's church in 1932; it was rebuilt in 2013. In 1991 the property was returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> over strong opposition from museum officials, and an order of 30 December 2004 transferred it to the Kostroma Diocese. In September 2002 one of the museum's main exhibits, a large wooden church of 1628 brought from the village of Spas-Vezhi, was destroyed by fire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ipatiev Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Ipatiev Monastery</b> near the town later played a part in the election of Michael Romanov in 1613.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kostroma">Principality of Kostroma</a></li></ul></section>
